# /{{VERB_SETUP}} — One-time bootstrap ({{ORG_NAME}})

`{{VERB_PRIME}}` reads the org brain every session; **`{{VERB_SETUP}}` is what puts the brain in place
the first time.** Run it once in a fresh working directory. After it succeeds, `{{VERB_PRIME}}`
takes over and you never need this again.

> **New to {{PLUGIN_NAME}}?** The entire getting-started path is: make a fresh empty
> directory, start Claude there, run `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_SETUP}}`, then
> `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}}`. No pre-existing `CLAUDE.md`, config, or inherited state
> needed — the wiki is the brain, and this skill fetches it right where you are.

## Invocation

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_SETUP}}
```

## What this skill does

Each step is idempotent — re-running is safe.

### 1. Clone the org brain into this directory

The brain is `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`. Clone it into the current working directory (wherever
the engineer started), so it lands right alongside their work:

```bash
test -f {{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}/CLAUDE.md \
  && { echo "{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}} already here — pulling latest"; git -C {{ORG_WIKI_NAME}} pull --ff-only; } \
  || git clone {{ORG_WIKI_REMOTE}} {{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}
```

### 2. Point the harness at it

The harness resolves the wiki via `${{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}` (falling back to
`{{ORG_WIKI_DEFAULT_PATH}}`). Set it to the clone you just made, so every future session
and skill finds it no matter where you launch:

```bash
WIKI_ABS="$(cd {{ORG_WIKI_NAME}} && pwd)"
export {{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}="$WIKI_ABS"
# persist it for future sessions:
echo "export {{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}=\"$WIKI_ABS\"" >> ~/.zshrc   # or ~/.bashrc
```

### 3. Scaffold the project repo index (multi-repo workspaces only)

If this working directory is a **multi-repo project workspace** (you'll clone several
repos here), give the project a top-level `CLAUDE.md` — its own **repo index**. This is
where the harness learns which repos the project spans. It lives **with the project,
not in the wiki** (project structure is per-project; the wiki is the reusable org
brain), and it auto-loads as context whenever you start a session at this top level.

Skip this for a single-repo project (just work inside that one repo).

If `./CLAUDE.md` doesn't already exist, scaffold one — list the repos you've cloned (or
will), one line each, with what each is for:

```bash
test -f ./CLAUDE.md || cat > ./CLAUDE.md <<'EOF'
# <project codename>

Multi-repo workspace for {{ORG_NAME}}. **Start {{PLUGIN_NAME}} sessions here** (the top
level) so this repo index loads as context.

## Repos (what this project spans)
<!-- one line per repo: name — what it's for. Keep current as the project grows. -->
- <repo-name>            — <role in this project>

## Operating model
Follows the {{ORG_NAME}} operating model — run /{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}} to load it from
{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}. Project *structure* lives here; org *process/knowledge* lives in the wiki.
EOF
```

Then edit the `## Repos` list to name the actual repos. `{{VERB_PRIME}}` reads this; `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}`
scopes each task to one of these repos.

### 4. Verify the tracker MCP is connected

{{PLUGIN_NAME}} drives the tracker through its MCP. Confirm the tools resolve; if not,
reconnect:

{{TRACKER_DOCTOR_SNIPPET}}

### 5. Confirm + hand off to prime

State, in a couple of lines: `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}` cloned here (✓), `{{ORG_WIKI_PATH_ENV}}`
set to it (✓), repo index present if multi-repo (✓), tracker MCP reachable (✓). Then:

```
Setup complete. From here on, just run /{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}} at the start of each
session — it reads the operating model + rules from the wiki, your repo index from the
workspace, and calibrates you.
```

## When to re-run

- Fresh machine or new working directory with no wiki → run `{{VERB_SETUP}}`.
- Wiki moved, or MCP stopped resolving → re-run; the steps are idempotent.

Otherwise you won't touch this — it's the one-time door, and `{{VERB_PRIME}}` is the daily one.

## Tool contract

`{{VERB_SETUP}}` clones the wiki (a one-time write to your local filesystem) and checks the MCP.
It does **not** write to the wiki, the tracker, or any repo. After it runs, `{{VERB_PRIME}}` owns
every session.

## Implementation note

Follow this SKILL.md as a procedure; it is not auto-executed.
